# Testing Guidance

When creating or updating tests, act like a pragmatic senior engineer. Keep the suite focused on behavior that can break, not implementation details or obvious framework behavior.

Use this prompt as guidance:

- Write small tests with clear assertions. Each test should prove one meaningful behavior or closely related set of behaviors.
- Combine cases that share the same setup when it improves readability, especially for validation matrices, role/permission variants, parser inputs, or similar edge cases.
- Avoid redundant "renders" or "initializes" tests when later interaction, state, or output tests already prove the component or unit renders.
- Do not test obvious language, framework, or library guarantees. Test project behavior, contracts, data mapping, side effects, and important edge cases.
- Prefer realistic inputs and outputs over checking private functions, internal state, CSS class names, or exact call order unless those details are the public contract.
- Keep mocks limited. Mock external services, network/database calls, time, randomness, expensive dependencies, and complex collaborators. Do not mock every local helper or simple dependency just to isolate code.
- Prefer shared setup helpers only when they remove real duplication. Keep setup close to the test when that makes intent easier to see.
- Use parameterized tests or table-driven cases where they make similar scenarios easier to scan.
- Name tests after the behavior or contract being protected, not the implementation method being exercised.
- Add new test files only when they fit the existing test layout. Otherwise extend the closest relevant test file.
